public class Solution {
    private int[] memo;

    public int lengthOfLIS(int[] nums) {
        int n = nums.length;
        memo = new int[n];
        Arrays.fill(memo, -1);

        int maxLIS = 1;
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            maxLIS = Math.max(maxLIS, dfs(nums, i));
        }
        return maxLIS;
    }

    private int dfs(int[] nums, int i) {
        if (memo[i] != -1) {
            return memo[i];
        }

        int LIS = 1;
        for (int j = i + 1; j < nums.length; j++) {
            if (nums[i] < nums[j]) {
                LIS = Math.max(LIS, 1 + dfs(nums, j));
            }
        }

        memo[i] = LIS;
        return LIS;
    }
}
//CODE END
# Longest Increasing Subsequence (LIS) - Top-Down DP Analysis

## Time Complexity: O(n^2)

* Subproblems: There are n distinct states (one for each index i from 0 to n - 1), where memo[i] represents the length of the longest increasing subsequence starting at index i.
* Work per Subproblem: When dfs(nums, i) is called for the first time, the for loop runs up to n - 1 - i times to check all indices j > i. Any recursive calls to dfs(nums, j) inside the loop either compute dfs(nums, j) once or return immediately in O(1) time from memo[j].
* Total Operations: Across all state evaluations, the sum of loop iterations across all i is:
  
  (n - 1) + (n - 2) + ... + 1 + 0 = n(n - 1) / 2 = O(n^2)

  The outer loop in lengthOfLIS calls dfs(nums, i) for each index, but since every dfs result is cached after its first run, each subproblem is computed exactly once.

---

## Space Complexity: O(n)

* Memoization Array: The memo array requires O(n) space to store results for indices 0 to n - 1.
* Recursion Call Stack: In the worst-case scenario (e.g., a strictly increasing array like [1, 2, 3, 4]), dfs recurses through every element sequentially, leading to a max call stack depth of O(n).
* Total Auxiliary Space: O(n) + O(n) = O(n)
